Step-by-Step Process

Step 1: Create EFS

	Go to:

		AWS EFS Console

	Create filesystem:

		Name: shared-efs
		VPC: Select your VPC
		Enable automatic backups (optional)


----------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 2: Configure Network

	EFS needs:

		Mount Targets
		Security Groups


	Create Security Group for EFS:

	Inbound Rules
	
		Type	Port	Source

		NFS		2049	EC2 Security Group

	Example:

		SG Name: efs-sg
		
	EC2 Security Group

	Allow outbound traffic to:

		Port 2049


--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 3: Create Mount Targets

	AWS automatically creates mount targets in subnets.

	Best practice:

		Create in every Availability Zone used by Auto Scaling.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 4: Launch EC2 and Install EFS Utilities

	Ubuntu
		sudo apt update
		sudo apt install nfs-common -y
	
	Amazon Linux
		sudo yum install -y amazon-efs-utils



-----------------------------------------------------------------



Step 5: Mount EFS

	Create mount directory:

		sudo mkdir /shared

	Mount EFS:

		sudo mount -t nfs4 \
		fs-xxxxxx.efs.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com:/ \
		/shared

	Verify:

		df -h



-----------------------------------------------------------------

Step 6: Make Permanent Mount

	Edit:

		sudo nano /etc/fstab

	Add:

		fs-xxxxxx.efs.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com:/ /shared nfs4 defaults,_netdev 0 0

	Test:

		sudo mount -a


--------------------------------------------------------------------



Step 7: Create Launch Template

	Go to:

		AWS EC2 Launch Templates

	Include:

		AMI
		Instance type
		Security groups
		IAM role


Step 8: Add User Data Script

	This is VERY IMPORTANT.

	Whenever Auto Scaling launches new EC2 instances,
	they automatically mount EFS.

		User Data Script (Ubuntu)
		#!/bin/bash

		apt update -y
		apt install -y nfs-common

		mkdir -p /shared

		mount -t nfs4 \
		fs-xxxxxx.efs.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com:/ \
		/shared

		echo "fs-xxxxxx.efs.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com:/ /shared nfs4 defaults,_netdev 0 0" >> /etc/fstab




Step 9: Create Auto Scaling Group

	Go to:

		AWS Auto Scaling Console

	Attach:

		Launch template
		Subnets
		Load balancer (optional)

	Configure:

		Min instances
		Max instances
		Desired capacity



Step 10: Test Auto Scaling

	Scale instances manually.

	Verify:

		ls /shared

	Create file in one server:

		echo "hello" > /shared/test.txt

	Check in another server:

		cat /shared/test.txt

	You will see the same file.





